  "docs.box.p1": "DO NOT OPEN is a collection of {supply} NFTs on Ethereum, built with Zama's encryption. Each token is a cardboard box. When a box is minted, the contract draws a random number for it and keeps that number encrypted. The cat inside, its state, its five traits and its rarity are all worked out from that one number. Who holds each box is encrypted too.",
  "docs.box.p2": "Encrypted here does not mean hidden behind a server or revealed later from a list someone prepared. The number is a ciphertext on-chain from the moment it exists. The contract can still compute with it: compare it, cut a piece out of it, add to it. It does that without ever decrypting it, which is what fully homomorphic encryption is for.",
  "docs.box.p3": "So the game is about what you choose to find out, and what that costs you. Shake a box and you learn one thing about it, privately. Open it and everyone learns everything, for good.",

  "docs.seed.p1": "A box holds a single 64-bit seed. Its bits are cut into seven fields. Sixteen decide whether the cat is alive, asleep, a ghost or quantum. Five bytes are the five traits. The last byte only changes how the cat looks.",
  "docs.seed.p2": "Keeping one encrypted number instead of eight keeps a mint cheap. Everything else is worked out from it when a mechanic needs it: under encryption while the box is sealed, in the clear once the box is opened. The app reads a seed exactly the way the contract does, so the cat you see is the cat the chain holds.",
  "docs.seed.p3": "The rarity score is a weighted sum: three times the breed roll, the mood roll, twice the accessory roll, the broken thing, the room, plus a bonus for the state. A quantum cat starts 1,000 points ahead. The highest possible score is {max}.",
  "docs.holders.p1": "The owner of each box is encrypted, like its seed. That is not how NFTs usually work: an ordinary NFT says publicly who owns it. Here nothing does. Nobody can see who holds a box, or count anyone's boxes. The collection follows a new standard written for this, the confidential ERC-721.",
  "docs.holders.p2": "Sending a box never fails because of who holds it. If you hold it, it moves; if you do not, nothing happens. Either way the sender and the receiver get an encrypted receipt that only they can read, saying whether the box moved, and everyone else sees a transfer that may or may not have happened. You find your own boxes the same way: the app reads the receipts that name you and decrypts them, with one signature per visit. That is what Show my boxes does.",
  "docs.holders.p3": "Minting works on the same idea. It is paid in cUSDC only, and your browser encrypts how many boxes you want. You also pick how many token ids to hide that number among, from 1 to {n}, and that choice is public. The first ids, up to your quantity, are yours; the others are empty boxes owned by nobody, and from outside the two look the same. More ids hide the quantity better and cost more gas. A mint that would pass the cap, or that your cUSDC balance does not cover, gets nothing and pays nothing.",
  "docs.holders.p4": "The number of boxes sold is an encrypted counter, capped at {saleCap} under encryption: of the {supply} boxes, the other {gifts} are kept for the whitelist's gifts, minted free when a seated wallet collects its gift, never sold. Only milestones of the sale are announced: {list}, the last one meaning sold out. The gift boxes count in none of them. Each mint makes public a single yes-or-no answer, whether the next milestone is reached, and anyone can announce it.",
  "docs.holders.p5": "Some things stay visible, by design. A mint shows the buyer's address and how many ids it created, which is an upper bound on what they bought. A transfer attempt shows who sent it and to whom, as a maybe. And an action that has to become public (an opening, an alive check, a box put up for a duel, or a duel or an entanglement that goes through) shows that its caller held that box at that moment. An attempt by someone who did not hold it settles as refused and shows nothing about the box. Croquettes add one more: the app only feeds boxes you hold, so a meal, whose event names the feeder, shows a box you hold, even though the contract treats a stranger's meal the same way. Claims do not: the app always claims for whole rows of ten box numbers, yours among others.",
  "docs.holders.p6": "This is also why the leaderboard ranks only opened cats, and the players who opened them: an opening publishes its opener, while who holds a sealed box stays hidden.",
  "docs.holders.p7": "One of those public facts reaches further. Once an action has shown that an address held a box, the next transfer of that box from that address is certainly real, not a maybe, so the box can be followed to its next holder, and from there onwards. When you give a box away, you can tick \"Send 3 decoys with it\": three more transfers of the same box to random addresses, which move nothing but look like the real one, sent in a random order with it. Then nobody can tell which one moved the box, or whether any did. Each costs gas. Without decoys, keep a box out of sight by receiving it, or moving it, at a fresh address you never use to prove anything.",

  "docs.privacy.p1": "Every ciphertext has an access list. For the seed, that list has one entry: the contract. No holder is ever added to it, because an address on the list could ask the key service to decrypt the seed directly and skip the game.",
  "docs.privacy.h.fact": "Fact",
  "docs.privacy.h.holder": "The holder",
  "docs.privacy.h.others": "Everyone else",
