  "docs.privacy.r2.holder": "Yes, free",
  "docs.privacy.r2.others": "Yes, by paying",
  "docs.privacy.r3": "Which trait a shake picked",
  "docs.privacy.r3.v": "Only whoever shook",
  "docs.privacy.r4": "Whether it is alive",
  "docs.privacy.r4.v": "Everyone, if the holder asks. One bit, once per box",
  "docs.privacy.r5": "Who won a duel, and one trait of the loser",
  "docs.privacy.r6": "The winner's trait in a duel",
  "docs.privacy.r7": "How many times it was petted, and how many meals it ate",
  "docs.privacy.r8": "How much affection that earned",
  "docs.privacy.r7.v": "Nobody. Attempts are public, not whether they counted",
  "docs.privacy.r9": "Who holds a box",
  "docs.privacy.r9.holder": "Yes, from their receipts",
  "docs.privacy.r10": "How many boxes an account holds",
  "docs.privacy.r10.holder": "Yes, for their own account",
  "docs.privacy.r11": "How many boxes are sold",
  "docs.privacy.r11.v": "Nobody. Only milestones are announced",
  "docs.privacy.p2": "Two consequences are deliberate. A holder who shakes often enough learns all five traits: a shake shows one of five at random, so about eleven cover them. What stays hidden even from the holder is the state, which is worth up to a third of the score, and the affection. And duels leak order: each one publishes which of two boxes scores higher. Putting a box up for a duel also shows that you hold it: that is how the duel shelf only carries boxes someone holds. That is the price of duelling.",

  "docs.flows.p1": "There are two ways out of encryption. A private decryption re-encrypts a value for one person, who must be on its access list. A public decryption publishes a value with signatures from the parties that share the key, and the contract verifies those signatures before it believes anything.",
  "docs.flows.p2": "The contract never receives a callback. A reveal is a request on-chain, a decryption off-chain, and a second transaction that carries the proof back. Anyone may send that second transaction. Every request also checks, under encryption, that its caller holds the box; that bit is decrypted with the rest, and a no settles the request as refused, with zeros in place of the box's values.",

