  "docs.mech.m4": "Pet",
  "docs.mech.m4.who": "Anyone",
  "docs.mech.m4.public": "That someone petted it, not whether it counted or what it earned",
  "docs.mech.m5": "Alive check",
  "docs.mech.m5.who": "The holder, once",
  "docs.mech.m5.public": "One bit, and that the caller held the box",
  "docs.mech.m6": "Entangle",
  "docs.mech.m6.who": "Both holders",
  "docs.mech.m6.public": "The link, and that both held their boxes. Opening one then opens both",
  "docs.mech.m7": "Duel",
  "docs.mech.m7.who": "The holder puts it up, any holder takes it up",
  "docs.mech.m7.public": "That the challenger holds the box, then who won and one trait of the loser, or a void duel",
  "docs.mech.m8": "Open",
  "docs.mech.m8.public": "Everything, permanently, and who opened it",
  "docs.mech.p1": "Petting adds a hidden amount between {min} and {max} to a hidden counter, and nothing at all if the pet was not paid. So the pets anyone can see on-chain say nothing about the counter, not even how many counted. Past {threshold}, the accessory turns golden when the box is opened, and a cat with no accessory gets a golden bell collar.",
  "docs.mech.p2": "Every price is in cUSDC, Zama's confidential USDC: nobody but you can read your balance or follow your payments, and the amount you paid is encrypted like the rest. A cUSDC payment cannot fail on-chain: a short balance just moves 0, and the action then does nothing. A mint gets no boxes, a paid shake reads \"not yours\", a pet adds nothing, an opening is refused. Paying from plain USDC means shielding the exact price into cUSDC first, in public, and for a mint that amount gives the quantity away. Shielding a round amount ahead of time, at the bureau de change, does not.",
  "docs.mech.p3": "Duels have a ranking of their own, in the leaderboard's Duels tab: boxes, not players, by duels won, then by fewest lost. The top three wear a rosette pinned to the front of their box, gold, silver and bronze, as at a cat show. A box can climb the podium and stay sealed: the ranking uses only the winners and losers that every duel already makes public, never who holds the box.",

  "docs.transfer.p1": "On this protocol an access grant cannot be revoked. A design that put the holder on the seed's access list could never take the seller off it. This one never puts anyone on, so a sale has nothing to undo on the seed. What changes hands is the encrypted owner, along with what waits in the box: paid-shake earnings and croquettes go with it.",
  "docs.transfer.t1": "Mint",
  "docs.transfer.t1.v": "Seed: the contract only. Owner: Alice, encrypted. The receipt: Alice only.",
  "docs.transfer.t2": "Alice shakes",
  "docs.transfer.t2.v": "A fresh ciphertext holding one trait: the contract and Alice.",
  "docs.transfer.t3": "Alice sells to Bob",
  "docs.transfer.t3.v": "She sends it. The owner changes under encryption, and the receipt, moved or not, is readable by Alice and Bob only. Nothing about the seed changes.",
  "docs.transfer.t4": "Bob shakes",
  "docs.transfer.t4.v": "Another fresh ciphertext: the contract and Bob.",
  "docs.transfer.t5": "Alice tries again",
  "docs.transfer.t5.v": "No error. She reads \"not yours\"; everyone else sees a shake, not that it failed. She keeps what she already saw, and gets nothing new.",
  "docs.transfer.t6": "Bob claims",
  "docs.transfer.t6.v": "The paid-shake earnings and croquettes waiting in the box go to him now, not to Alice.",
  "docs.transfer.p2": "If you buy a sealed box, assume the seller knows its five traits. A few paid shakes before buying level that. And a transfer that names you is only a maybe until you decrypt its receipt: Show my boxes does it for you.",
  "docs.section.studio": "The studio",
  "docs.section.terms": "The release form",
  "docs.terms.p1": "The first time you connect a wallet, the game hands you a release form, Form DNO-1: the terms of play, eight short clauses. They say plainly what you are walking into. The contracts are experimental software: nobody has audited them, and a bug, an exploit or a hack could lose what you put in, without the creators being liable as far as the law allows. The website is only a window: your boxes, cats and balances live on-chain, so if the site breaks, nothing of yours is lost. Nobody holds your money for you, nothing is refunded, and none of this is an investment.",
  "docs.terms.p2": "You initial each clause, one click each, then sign with your wallet. The signature is free and sends no transaction: your wallet shows a readable message naming your address, the version of the terms and a fingerprint of the full English text. The translations are there to read; the English text is what you sign. The signature is kept as a record of your acceptance, by the game's server when it is reachable, and always in your browser. Without a wallet, nothing is asked: you can look around the whole site, and the form only comes up when a wallet that has not signed it connects. You can close it to keep looking around, or switch to another wallet from it; any action you then ask for (a mint, a shake, a feed) brings it back until it is signed.",
  "docs.terms.p3": "Each new wallet signs once, and a new version of the terms is signed again before playing on. To read the form again and see your signature, open the menu and choose Terms of play. On the test network nothing is signed: the form waits for mainnet, and a notice says instead what an update of the contracts keeps (your points, your whitelist seat, your X pass).",
  "docs.testnet.note": "This part goes away at mainnet.",
  "docs.testnet.p1": "The game runs on Sepolia, Ethereum's test network. Nothing here is worth money. The USDC is Zama's test token, free from the wallet slip or the bureau de change; the ETH for gas comes from a faucet; the croquette pool only exists so the market can be tried; and every price in this manual is a test price.",
  "docs.testnet.allowList": "The mainnet allow list has {places} seats, first come, first served: a seat goes to each player who claims one, or who connects an X account on the boarding page, until none is left. Your points: 3 for each opponent you beat in a duel, 1 for each opponent you faced, 2 for each box you opened, up to 10 boxes. A wallet linked to your X boarding pass gets {xBonus} more, and {discordBonus} more again once you joined the Discord server and ran /board there with the code the boarding page gives you. Each friend who boards from your referral link adds {refBonus} more, up to {refCap} friends, once their pass holds a seat with a wallet linked. Each opponent counts once, however many times you meet, and duels between your own wallets count nothing. Claim your place from the leaderboard's Duels tab by signing with your wallet: it is free and sends no transaction, and it tells the game only that your address is asking. Sign again later to update your points; the best you ever had are kept, even if the test network starts over. Claimants are ranked by points; between equal points, the earlier claim goes first.",
  "docs.testnet.gifts": "Every seat comes with a gift, set by your rank among the seated players when the list closes. Ranks 1 to {firstTo} fly First class: {firstMin} to {firstMax} cCROQ, a sealed box and a rat. Up to rank {businessTo}, Business: {businessMin} to {businessMax} cCROQ and a box. Up to rank {economyTo}, Economy: {economyMin} to {economyMax} cCROQ and a rat. The croquettes are drawn at random under encryption: only your wallet can read how many. The box is minted free for you out of the boxes kept outside the sale, so a sold-out sale never empties a gift; the rat is a free one, its seed drawn at random. Both are public, like any box's first holder after a mint of one. A gift nobody collects is never minted. The gift is collected on mainnet, in one transaction from the same Duels tab, within {days} days of the list closing, by a wallet that claimed its place: a seat held only by an X account has no wallet to send it to.",
  "docs.testnet.contracts": "The contracts on Sepolia:",
